{"author":{"id":"5698b6d16d951325374257d86a2e673da0e55970c3d21c0f7c6f49018f24e5bf"},"ops":[{"type":3,"timestamp":1787151801,"nonce":"R6zDa55v3mDRpWqcaIva4QHqvr8=","message":"**Yes, these three are one design, and the shared root is wider than \"the cursor is a boundary rather than an instant\".** This ticket says *\"choosing is the work\"*, and three unattended sessions have now declined to choose, correctly. This comment exists so the choice takes five minutes rather than a re-derivation.\n\n**Ticket hygiene, checked first:** none of `893cee4`, `342e071`, `a8a4010` or `78388fb` has been superseded by a later comment — each carries exactly one entry, its own body — and all three failure modes reproduce as described against the current code. One citation nit only: `a8a4010` attributes the clock read at `process.go:182` to `plan()`, and that line is actually inside `normalise`, which `plan` calls. The mechanism it describes is unaffected. Worth stating because this repo has three tickets whose bodies a later comment overturned, so \"the body still governs\" is a finding, not an absence of one.\n\n**The root.** `digest_window_start` on the last-sent digest row is one fact asked to answer three different questions, and it cannot answer all three: *was this span examined at all*, *how far did the examination reach in calendar time independently of window length*, and *was the examination's data complete*.\n\n- `342e071` is the boundary-versus-instant bug exactly. A boundary re-floored under a different `digest_window_s` means something else; an instant does not change meaning when the tiling changes.\n- `a8a4010` is that plus one more assumption: even an instant is wrong if it is `now`, because `now` is not *\"everything with a `started_at` before now is visible in this transaction\"*. It needs an instant that accounts for commit latency.\n- `893cee4` needs the reframing **plus a decoupling**, and this is the part the one-sentence framing misses. The cursor is only ever written as a side effect of SENDING, so *\"examined and found quiet\"* and *\"never examined\"* produce an identical absence. Making the cursor an instant does not fix that on its own — if the instant still only advances inside `emit`, a quiet window still leaves it frozen. **Coverage and \"a digest was sent\" have to become two separately recorded facts.**\n\n**The designs, each a whole answer rather than a third of one:**\n\n- **(a) coverage-reached instant, advanced UNCONDITIONALLY every tick** to `WindowStart(now − safety_lag)`, as a new fact distinct from the sent row. Fixes all three. Needs a migration. ⭐ The codebase already ships this exact pattern: `internal/streaming/service/bridge.go` has a `DefaultLagWindow = 10 * time.Second` documented as *\"the fix for the commit-order race\"* between a watermark and Postgres commit visibility. Testable with the existing fake clock.\n- **(b) the same, but the instant is a Postgres transaction-visibility watermark** rather than a clock lag. Removes the guesswork in sizing the lag and is the exact rather than heuristic fix for `a8a4010` — but it is unprecedented here, needs a real database to test, and a stuck long-running transaction freezes the watermark. It still needs (a)'s unconditional advance to fix the other two.\n- **(c) stamp the Case at commit** rather than before the write. Surgically exact for `a8a4010` and does nothing for the other two. Risky in isolation: `started_at` is read across `internal/alerts` for episode duration and timeline ordering, and `lifecycle.go` is explicit that `recorded_at` and `occurred_at` are *\"NEVER conflated\"*.\n- **(d) keep the boundary cursor, make the query overlap-tolerant with per-Case dedupe.** The only option needing no instant concept, and the most invasive: it discards the property `repository/digest.go:139-144` names as the point of the current design — *\"the digest row IS the cursor… the only state that can exist is state a digest was actually sent for\"* — and it replaces `notif_digest_uniq`'s window-shaped idempotency key. Right only if a digest should stop being \"a window\" at all, which is a bigger decision than these tickets ask for.\n\n**`skipped_windows` under (a):** the honest answer is that it stops being a backlog. Once coverage advances every tick, a quiet policy has nothing outstanding, so the number is either deleted or redefined as *\"windows examined and found empty\"* — which is a diagnostic, not a debt. Deciding which is part of the same choice.\n\n**Recommendation: (a).** It is the only design that closes all three at once, it reuses a pattern already shipped in this repo, and it is testable offline. What would change my mind: evidence that plan-to-commit latency has a long, variable tail under load, which would argue for pairing it with (b). That is unmeasured — nothing in the code answers it.\n\n⭐ **The one question only you can answer:** how large should `safety_lag` be, or should oto use a transaction-visibility watermark instead of a fixed lag? Everything else follows mechanically.\n\n**`78388fb` is correctly blocked behind this** and its ordering was right: it wants to render a digest's span, and the span's length is not stored anywhere today. Under (a) the digest row gains a coverage fact it can render, instead of inferring the span from the policy's CURRENT config — which is precisely what `342e071` shows is unsafe.\n\nFull memo with every `file:line`, the three failure modes traced through the code, and the design table: `~/.afk/oto/20260819-1920/decision-digest-cursor.md`.\n\n⚠️ **Nothing built and nothing decided.** An unattended session must not pick a product axis.","files":null}]}